  body: "# Scope of the hospital guidance after the 2006 change\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe source
    records the end of hospital downrating under the revoked regulations from 10 April 2006 and describes
    the remaining chapter uses for AA/DLA in-patient meaning and specified dependency-increase questions.\n\
    \n## Scope and exceptions\n\nThis is a dated source account, not a claim that every benefit is unaffected
    by hospital admission today. DMG 18000 routes IS/JSA and Pension Credit to their own chapters; older
    material remains in chapter 18. Each benefit, period and retained exception requires review.\n\n##
    Inspected source passages\n\n- [DMG 18000, PDF page 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a80d88aed915d74e33fcbde/dmgch18.pdf#page=3)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 18: Hospital in-patients.\n- [DMG 18001, PDF page 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a80d88aed915d74e33fcbde/dmgch18.pdf#page=3)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 18: Hospital in-patients.\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted interpretation
    of selected passages in the 15 September 2026 frozen source snapshot. Specialist review is required.
    Source acquisition, source revision, legal effective dates and this record’s authoring time are separate.\n\
    \nThis is an independent experimental publication, not an official DWP document, individual advice,
    an entitlement decision or an executable rule. Machine-extracted text may alter reading order, footnotes
    and characters. No PDF visual inspection, whole-chapter coverage or behavioural evaluation pass is
    claimed by this record.\n\n- `dmg-vol3-ch18`: PDF SHA-256 `cb377d4db23653485a8a4c10e1ea19da1bba9bb9aad731e382e22b53fc4fa863`;
    extracted pages SHA-256 `7953f5a17805c8271ec4bcc926e0414ba0b19c358597faaf4eee431006a456a8`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/common-hospital-individual-treatment
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The remaining in-patient meaning depends on individual treatment facts and multiple
      conditions, rather than the establishment’s label alone.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch18/0003
      quote: "18001   This Chapter therefore now only gives guidance on when a person is to be regarded\n\
        \        as a hospital in-patient for the purposes of AA and DLA plus some minor matters\n   \
        \     relating to the payability of ADIs and CDIs when the claimant or both the claimant\n   \
        \     and the dependant have been hospital in-patients for 52 weeks or more.\n\n        18002
        - 18009\n\n\n\n\nVol 3 Amendment 28 October 2008"
      locator: DMG 18001
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch18/0007
      quote: "18020   There are five points to consider when deciding whether a person is receiving free\n\
        \        in-patient treatment. The person must be all of the following\n\n        1.     maintained
        free of charge\n\n        2.     receiving medical or other treatment\n\n        3.     in a hospital
        or similar institution\n\n        4.     an in-patient\n\n        5.    receiving free in-patient
        treatment under prescribed legislation1 or in a\n              hospital or similar institution
        maintained or administered by the Defence\n              Council.\n\n        Note: A person who
        does not meet any one of these conditions is not receiving free\n        in-patient treatment.
        The decision maker does not need to consider the other\n        conditions.\n                \
        \                               1 NHS Act 06; NHS (Wales) Act 06 NHS (Scot) Act 78; NHS & CC Act
        90\n\n\n\n        Maintained free of charge"
      locator: DMG 18020
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch18/0008
      quote: "18025   Whether a person is receiving treatment is a question of fact. It cannot be assumed\n\
        \        that because one person is receiving treatment in a hospital or similar\n        establishment,
        that another person in the same establishment can automatically be\n        regarded as receiving
        treatment. But the fact that a person is an in-patient in a\n                                \
        \                                                                          1\n        hospital
        is strong evidence that the person is receiving medical or other treatment .\n               \
        \                                                                                   1 R(S) 26/54"
      locator: DMG 18025
  - target: term/jsais-free-inpatient-treatment
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The chapter expressly sends IS and JSA hospital treatment to chapter 24; this is a
      research route, not an assertion that all hospital rules are equivalent.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch18/0003
      quote: "18000   With effect from 10.4.06 the old Hospital In-Patients Regulations1 were revoked2.\n\
        \        The result was that hospital downrating ceased to apply to the benefits to which\n  \
        \      those regulations applied (see the list in DMG 18010).\n\n        Note: For guidance on
        the effect of hospitalization on IS and JSA see DMG Vol 4\n        Chapter 24 and for SPC see
        Vol 13 Chapter 78.\n                                                           1 SS (HIP) Regs
        75; 2 SS (HIP) Regs 05, reg 9"
      locator: DMG 18000
